## Description

A CASE label (or range) that repeats values of an earlier label in the same CASE: only the first branch is
taken for those values.

## Rationale

`CASE nStep OF 10: � 5..15: �` � step 10 never reaches the second branch. Usually a copied step number in a
state machine. Whether a compiler rejects an exact duplicate depends on the vendor and version; overlapping ranges
are accepted more often.

## Noncompliant

```st
PROGRAM P_Test
VAR
    nLevel : INT;
    bPump : BOOL;
    bAlarm : BOOL;
END_VAR
CASE nLevel OF
    0: bPump := FALSE;
    10: bPump := TRUE;
    5..15: bAlarm := TRUE;
ELSE
    bAlarm := FALSE;
END_CASE
END_PROGRAM
```

## Compliant

```st
PROGRAM P_Test
VAR
    nLevel : INT;
    bPump : BOOL;
    bAlarm : BOOL;
END_VAR
CASE nLevel OF
    0: bPump := FALSE;
    10: bPump := TRUE;
    11..15: bAlarm := TRUE;
ELSE
    bAlarm := FALSE;
END_CASE
END_PROGRAM
```

## Description

Every branch of an IF with ELSE (or a CASE with ELSE) ends with the same statement, or starts with it: write it
once after (or before) the block.

## Rationale

Duplicated statements drift apart when one copy is edited. "Starts with" is reported only when the statement
writes nothing the conditions read, so moving it before the block keeps the meaning. Off by default.

## Noncompliant

```st
PROGRAM P_Test
VAR
    nLevel : INT;
    bPump : BOOL;
    bAlarm : BOOL;
END_VAR
IF nLevel > 10 THEN
    bPump := TRUE;
    bAlarm := FALSE;
ELSE
    bPump := FALSE;
    bAlarm := FALSE;
END_IF
END_PROGRAM
```

## Compliant

```st
PROGRAM P_Test
VAR
    nLevel : INT;
    bPump : BOOL;
    bAlarm : BOOL;
END_VAR
IF nLevel > 10 THEN
    bPump := TRUE;
ELSE
    bPump := FALSE;
END_IF
bAlarm := FALSE;
END_PROGRAM
```
